hi this is dr. Gregory Sadler I'm a  professor of philosophy and the  president and founder of an educational  consulting company called reason IO  where we put philosophy into practice  I've studied and taught philosophy for  over 20 years and I find that many  people run into difficulties reading  classic philosophical texts sometimes  it's the way things are said or how the  text is structured but the concepts  themselves are not always that  complicated and that's where I come in  to help students and lifelong learners  I've been producing longer lecture  videos and posting them to youtube many  viewers say they find them useful what  you're currently watching is part of a  new series of shorter videos each of  them focused on one core concept from an  important philosophical text I hope you  find it useful as well in articulating  for us his theory of moral development  Lawrence Kohlberg sets out a number of  distinct stages of moral development and  he also tells us that he's more  interested in form rather than content  when it comes to moral reasoning now a  lot of the early work that he did was  with school children but he also worked  with in prisons and he would carry out  these experiments where he would set up  a dilemma and I'll tell you about the  dilemma in a moment and then he would  ask people to say what is the right  answer here and then to give some sort  of justification for their answer to  explain a little bit further and the  situation has to do with this guy Hans  and you could do with other situations  as well other moral dilemmas as a  particularly good case Hans has a wife  who's sick and the pharmacist will not  allow her to have the very expensive  medicine because Hans does not have the  money to pay for it the question is  should Hans then break into the pharmacy  and  take the medicine which will allow his  wife to continue her life or should he  not do that which is the right thing to  do but more importantly why and this  goes directly to this issue of form and  and content for when content not just in  terms of our thoughts or our verbal  expressions but also in terms of our  actions and this is what makes  Kohlberg's theory so interesting for  Ethics besides the classification of the  six stages he's very interested in what  we would call practical reasoning that  is the the way of using our Faculty of  Reason along with our other faculties to  arrive at decisions and judgments about  what is good and what ought to be done  or conversely what is bad and what ought  to be avoided now if we think about this  in terms of form and content content he  tells us in moral development a review  of the theory really encompasses two  main things the first is what people  think is right or wrong and he's not  quite so interested in that on its own  for its own thing again form and content  content matters because there's a form  that gives that content its its location  its context what people think is right  or wrong by itself is not that important  or interesting  what's important is what they do with  that and how they arrive at that  likewise she says they're behavioral  responses by themselves you know which  would include actions but could also  include say attitudes maybe even we  could talk about you know the the  decisions that are made are kind of a  behavioral response as well or somewhere  in between behavioral and cognitive  those two are just content so what is  the form  and he takes us actually from from John  Dewey who he brings up along with Piaget  he says like Piaget Dewey's idea of  development does not reflect an increase  in the content of thinking but instead  of qualitative transformation in the  form of the child's thought or action he  says what we examine in our work has to  do with form rather than content right  we are not thinking or describing what  people think is right or wrong whether  draft evading exiles should be given  amnesty or you know thrown into jail  when they return to this country or even  what what changes in what individuals  think as they grow older and here he's  referring to another one of his his own  articles he says that what we're looking  at here is cognitive structures  cognitive structures underlying what  people think is right and wrong  cognitive structures underlying  behavioral responses the thing that  actually connects all of these together  and what we can observe when we ask  people why do you think that's right or  wrong why do you feel the way that you  do and they explained it to us so he  goes on and he says for example this  this structure refers to the general  characteristics of shaped pattern or  organization of response rather than the  rate of intensity of response or its  pairing with particular system stimuli  it refers to rules for processing  information or for connecting experience  of events now how do we translate all  that jargon into you know moral theory  really what we're talking about here is  how a person carries out his or her  moral reasoning and decision making  whether it's their own whether it's a  proxy for somebody else whether it's  about some hypothetical case that is put  in front of them this is the form the  thought process that they go go through  and then set down in words so that we  can  see it it also includes what they are  referencing in explaining or justifying  their views how they're taking the  things that they value how they're  taking the things that they think matter  or right and wrong how they're taking  those into the situation and connecting  them up with the ultimate need to say  yes or no right or wrong do this don't  do that now at different levels like for  example at level one you can ask a  person why did you do that or why didn't  you do that action or why should a  person not do that action or in the case  of Hans why should Hans either steal or  not steal the medicine and at a level  one they might say well you know Hans  shouldn't steal the medicine because  he'll be punished the the police will  come and beat him up and throw him in  jail and it won't do his wife any good  but it's really bad for Hans to go in  jail cuz he'll they'll hurt him there  well that's a level one that's a an  explanation of why he shouldn't do that  that thing you know at level four there  might be another thing well listen the  rules are the rules it's unfortunate  that Hans doesn't have any money but we  can't have people going around breaking  into everybody's pharmacies when they  need you know some sort of thing that  they can't pay for society can't take  that we have to protect society that's a  level four justification and that is a  different form for a similar content the  similar content being Hans keep your  hands off different explanation for why  that's the case similarly if we take a  level three response and then maybe a  level six response hans loves his wife  so much that he will break in and that's  the right thing for him to do because he  doesn't really care that much about the  shopkeeper that's his neighbor but he  really loves his wife and he  wants his wife to stay alive and for  them to be happy together that's all  level three response right Hans is a  nice guy he was a good guy and he has a  nice girl that's a level three  orientation what about if we put it up  to level five or level six maybe at a  level six you could say well Hans  recognizes that he cannot sacrifice the  humanity within his wife merely to  uphold you know values important to  society like not breaking people's  windows and taking their stuff human  life has a priority over mere material  right we invoke something like a conch  in distinction between what has genuine  value and what only has the value that  we call trade or use value right some  things have cost other things in dignity  that is a level six form response but  both the level three and the level six  response saying Hans Brandt get in there  take the medicine give it to your wife  both of them are working with the exact  same content so we can have the same  content and in a different form and the  form is what determines whether a person  is in fact at one stage of moral  development or at another so that is  what Kohlberg thinks we need to focus  upon  you
 

